  "response": "The polywater controversy was settled less by a single decisive experiment than by the steady accumulation of analytical chemistry against a structural hypothesis. Its origin lies in the Soviet Union, where Nikolai Fedyakin first reported anomalous water formed in narrow capillaries in 1962. The substance reached wide attention after Boris Derjaguin, a distinguished surface chemist at the Institute of Physical Chemistry in Moscow, took up the work and championed it at international meetings. Derjaguin's reputation gave the claim a credibility that a single provincial report would never have commanded, and laboratories in Britain and the United States soon began attempting to reproduce the material.\n\nThe setting and the method should be distinguished. Fedyakin conducted his original anomalous-water experiments at a laboratory in Leningrad. By contrast, the material itself was defined entirely by its mode of preparation: anomalous water was produced by condensing water vapor into very fine quartz or glass capillaries. The yields from that procedure were minute, often micrograms of liquid isolated in columns only a few micrometres wide. Such quantities strained the analytical techniques of the period and left investigators reliant on a narrow set of physical measurements, chiefly density, viscosity, freezing behaviour and vibrational spectroscopy.\n\nEllis Lippincott and colleagues published infrared spectra of anomalous water in 1969, seven years after Fedyakin's initial report. Lippincott's 1969 polywater spectra were published in the journal Nature. That venue confirms that Lippincott's group interpreted their spectra as evidence of a polymeric structure of water molecules, since the paper laid out a model of hexagonal units bound by unusually strong, symmetric hydrogen bonds. Whereas that spectroscopic reading proposed a specific molecular architecture, the substantive question facing chemists was simpler and admitted only two answers: either polywater was a genuinely new polymeric phase of water, or polywater was not a genuinely new polymeric phase of water. The two possibilities could not both be true, and one of them had to hold, so the task became one of deciding which. That question was eventually decided by the international community of chemists, which determined that polywater was not a genuinely new polymeric phase of water.\n\nDespite the polymeric interpretation, chemical analyses of polywater samples detected substantial amounts of silicon, sodium, and other impurities. Electron microprobe and related techniques, applied to the few samples available, showed that the liquid was far from pure water. A second line of inquiry came from Bell Laboratories, where Denis Rousseau reported that the infrared spectrum of polywater closely resembled that of human sweat; he obtained the comparison spectrum from his own perspiration after a handball game. The impurity measurements indicate that the anomalous properties of polywater samples arose from silica dissolved from the capillary walls, or they arose from organic contaminants such as sweat-derived compounds. At least one of these contamination routes operated in any given sample, perhaps both, depending on how the capillaries had been cleaned and handled. Freshly drawn silica glass is readily attacked by condensed water films, while capillaries manipulated by hand accumulate lipids and salts that concentrate in the tiny condensate volumes.\n\nThe retreat of the principal advocate followed. Derjaguin retracted his claims about anomalous water in 1973; that is, in 1973 he publicly withdrew his assertion that anomalous water was a new phase of water. His own group, after rigorous cleaning of the capillaries and careful exclusion of contaminants, found that the anomalous properties disappeared. The scientific community ultimately concluded that polywater was an artifact of contamination, and this conclusion settles the tension between Lippincott's polymeric reading of the spectra and the impurity data, because the spectral features once attributed to novel bonding were fully accounted for by dissolved silicates, sodium salts and organic residues. The episode is now taught as a case in which analytical chemistry, rather than structural theorizing, determined the outcome: once the composition of the samples was measured directly, the case for a new phase of water could no longer be sustained.",
